.TH EXTRACT_ARCHIVES 1 "2026-11-08" "extract_archives.sh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
extract_archives.sh \- extract archives at limited depth and remove the originals
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B extract_archives.sh
[\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fIPATH\fR...]
.br
.B extract_archives.sh
\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B extract_archives.sh
resolves each
.I PATH
argument to a list of archives, verifies their integrity, extracts each one
into a new subdirectory named after the archive (without its extension), and
deletes the original archive only after a fully successful extraction.
.P
A
.I PATH
that is a directory is searched for archives up to
.I N
levels deep (default 1, i.e. only archives directly inside the directory).
A
.I PATH
that is a regular file is treated as an archive and processed directly,
without any directory scan; this is how callers hand over a single known
archive. With no
.I PATH
the current directory (\fB.\fR) is searched as a directory.
.P
Archives that fail their integrity check or fail to extract are left in
place and reported at the end; the partially extracted target directory of a
failed extraction is removed.
.P
Supported formats are detected from the extension, which is lowercased
before matching, so uppercase extensions such as
.BR .ZIP ", " .TAR.GZ ", and " .7Z
are handled. A file passed explicitly triggers no extension scan and is
reported as skipped if its format is unsupported.
.P
Supported formats:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar.gz\fR and \fB.tgz\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar.bz2\fR and \fB.tbz2\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar.xz\fR and \fB.txz\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.zip\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.7z\fR
.P
Each archive is extracted with the matching tool:
.BR tar (1)
for all
.B tar
variants,
.BR unzip (1)
for
.BR .zip ,
and
.BR 7z (1)
for
.BR .7z .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I PATH...
One or more directories to search and/or archive files to process.
Directories are scanned recursively to depth
.IR N ;
files are processed as-is. Default:
.BR . .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-depth " N"
Process archives up to
.I N
directories deep when scanning directories. Default: 1. Must match
.IR ^[1\-9][0-9]*$ ;
anything else exits 1.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS Integrity check
Before extraction, every archive is tested:
.IP \(bu 2
.B zip:
.B unzip \-qt
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar.gz:
.B gzip \-t
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar.bz2:
.B bzip2 \-t
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar.xz:
.B xz \-t
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar:
.B tar \-tf
.IP \(bu 2
.B 7z:
.B 7z t
.P
If the check fails, the archive is reported as failed and kept untouched.
.SS Extraction
.P
The archive is extracted into
.IR dir/name ,
where
.I dir
is the directory containing the archive and
.I name
is the archive filename with its known extension removed (e.g.
.I foo.tar.gz
is extracted to
.IR foo/ ).
For
.BR .zip
archives the tool is
.BR unzip\ \-o\ \-q ,
for
.B tar
variants
.BR tar\ \-xf\ \-C ,
and for
.BR .7z
.BR 7z\ x\ \-o .
.P
If extraction fails, the partial target directory is removed with
.BR rm\ \-rf
and the archive is reported as failed and kept.
.P
On success the original archive is deleted with
.BR rm\ \-f .
.SH EXAMPLES
Extract archives in the current directory:
.IP
.EX
extract_archives.sh
.EE
.P
Process a specific directory two levels deep:
.IP
.EX
extract_archives.sh \-d 2 ~/Downloads
.EE
.P
Extract two specific archives and ignore everything else in their directory:
.IP
.EX
extract_archives.sh ~/Downloads/one.zip ~/Downloads/two.7z
.EE
.P
Typical output for a clean run:
.IP
.EX
Processing: ~/Downloads/backup.tar.gz
  [OK] Integrity verified
  [DONE] Extracted and removed: ~/Downloads/backup.tar.gz

All archives processed successfully.
.EE
.P
Typical output when an archive cannot be read:
.IP
.EX
Processing: ~/Downloads/corrupt.zip
  [FAIL] Integrity check failed, keeping archive: ~/Downloads/corrupt.zip

===== FAILED ARCHIVES (1) =====
  - ~/Downloads/corrupt.zip
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run completed; individual extraction failures are reported on stdout in
the failed-archives summary, not via the exit status.
.TP
.B 1
Bad
.B \-d
value, unknown option, or a
.I PATH
that does not exist.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qbt-extract (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR unzip (1),
.BR 7z (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR flock (1)
